#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a28c4320-e2dd-5195-81ac-b910c2328691" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100fac5f-cc12-56dc-9567-ee01010ba781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408152} {:answer/id #uuid "1377aca7-a017-58fe-8e23-256c042e11b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408153} {:answer/id #uuid "3aabdbf2-aad9-5028-8b69-34e437771408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408154} {:answer/id #uuid "41ea5617-4c16-5bdc-a231-c9432f86dc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408155} {:answer/id #uuid "93d7f804-0816-5794-84e7-a2fe566f8c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aabdbf2-aad9-5028-8b69-34e437771408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408154} :answer-field/id #uuid "85ef2574-1c06-549a-a456-4b22b82491c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffe5914e-4912-5ff3-9b15-3a1462960003" :question/math-academy-id "q-132285" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\\,dx=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}]_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+1-{x}^{4}-{x}^{4})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{4})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{4})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x-\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+1-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a28c4320-e2dd-5195-81ac-b910c2328691" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d2a009-9a40-5d25-99e2-4f97af963231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321198} {:answer/id #uuid "2c533ee8-f9db-5f4a-a560-8035da471b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321496} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0552d2-cfa6-5c1e-901c-be3cad62fa64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321596} {:answer/id #uuid "70ec84ef-6655-53bf-9eb0-7897a3753db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322036} {:answer/id #uuid "80d50f5a-f000-539b-a1db-5c8a13952505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80d50f5a-f000-539b-a1db-5c8a13952505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322166} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b2db87a-5385-53da-8e27-89a6fe772ff2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39356ac1-0a31-5e6e-9a6f-6a96bbbbe55c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37683" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{x}^{1/x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}-x]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{1}{3}+1-(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{11}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69f6b2a7-7a3a-5c2c-90db-5aa321784048" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b726bfa-ba4c-5d91-9f6d-6a3764d8755d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321248} {:answer/id #uuid "1594e17d-7011-5f7d-aa6a-12336ec25a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321328} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5f94c5-0d1b-5f79-944c-8cb9d821beed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321485} {:answer/id #uuid "564621d9-f224-5fff-b159-cf3615e51a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321834} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f698ad-bda0-5b89-8175-c64721d84bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b5f94c5-0d1b-5f79-944c-8cb9d821beed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321485} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb45d35-7041-5bf4-8524-44adec58ddbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad4a8e76-c297-5cff-8131-747df131b332" :question/math-academy-id "q-37865" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line\n$y=1-x$ and the coordinate axes." :question/worked-solution "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/95/95ebd0a4eb874972b459e53eecf1d57650f63284698fcad967c09620ac01cd99.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=1-x$ intercepts the $x$-axis at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/d0/d048f20f2299722cee598cc1005237627ecdd7643e996b269f26b5a096f01f2f.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{-x+1}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{1}{1+x+y}]}_{0}^{-x+1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{1+x+(1-x)}-(-\\frac{1}{1+x+0}))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-(0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853b2770-5057-50aa-9c15-7426cf4cc6a4" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f2f8c3-eca9-558a-ae7f-9a78208457ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408156} {:answer/id #uuid "647e13d7-fe24-5171-9f25-59459873bf42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408157} {:answer/id #uuid "93175eb3-2514-5cc0-a423-e088c96ac28d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408158} {:answer/id #uuid "93b1f2e7-cf7b-5b8a-b5bb-c62e89e19fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408159} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3be0a4-83b9-54e6-81b8-88c21e425fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-1}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93b1f2e7-cf7b-5b8a-b5bb-c62e89e19fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408159} :answer-field/id #uuid "3df51106-2408-5663-8451-c957471724da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3676ec52-02c6-5b83-a547-e552c5ceb042" :question/math-academy-id "q-81338" :question/problem "![](images/41/4121d975786de30f34e493e7ac49e5cdc8e66621e800321008ba4ba501fb818f.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=-{x}^{2},$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-1\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/ef/ef6450f795d0edea8a3217e28d7f1a966d5b1c62f9241826213de0485af5f860.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le -{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69f6b2a7-7a3a-5c2c-90db-5aa321784048" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7ddc79-cbfd-59b5-9638-d178b71104a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348542} {:answer/id #uuid "4b79160f-37ab-5192-8514-437d9b279188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349144} {:answer/id #uuid "544819eb-b6d1-5a40-bb3a-d34d78eef696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349224} {:answer/id #uuid "a06fce6a-f06f-595a-9863-76102c8d8305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349877} {:answer/id #uuid "f06aa1a1-4329-543e-8ec1-01bf70f9f867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a06fce6a-f06f-595a-9863-76102c8d8305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349877} :answer-field/id #uuid "08077c01-ccff-5e05-a424-ef1243bb7696" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b8f9d362-d296-527d-b3eb-8711496d6537" :question/math-academy-id "q-81367" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines\n$x=e,$ $y=e$ and the curve $y={e}^{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/aa/aa635ca5ef3198fea56d684b034de476a72e4ace2a4c51b37c6c59977036eb76.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=e$ intercepts the curve $y={e}^{x}$ at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$1\\le x\\le e.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/b7/b78021a06c9fea100b75c66a619c425345cda3666d84da018895be15a53af06d.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dA={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)]}_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx & ={[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡e}{2}-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
